// SHA-3 and SHAKE, bound to Markku-Juhani O. Saarinen's tiny_sha3.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because tiny_sha3 depends on nothing: `sha3.c` includes `sha3.h`,
// which includes `<stddef.h>` and `<stdint.h>`, and that is the whole list. Both files are carried in
// this package, because tiny_sha3 is not a library anybody has installed.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       sha3 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/sha3", version = "0.1.1" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.1` on this repository.

package {
  name    = "sha3"
  version = "0.1.1"

  // The compiler this was built and verified against. The raw-form import (`sysl.raw.ptr_cast`)
  // does not exist on 0.5.x.
  sysl = "0.6.0"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block, and in this package the claim is unusually strong.**
//
// A capability clause says what a package asks of *sysl*, and what tiny_sha3 does is arithmetic on
// bytes it was handed. It opens no file, reads no clock, calls no allocator -- and, unlike every other
// vendored-C package in this organisation, reaches for no C library function at all. There is no
// `memcpy`, no `assert`, no `strlen`: the implementation copies with explicit loops, which is what
// makes the include list two headers long.
//
// **So here `requires {}` also means it compiles for a bare target, which is a separate question and
// usually a different answer.** qrcodegen and miniz make the same capability claim and still fail on
// `--target thumb-freestanding`, because clang gives a freestanding target `stdint.h` and `stddef.h`
// and not `assert.h`, `stdlib.h` or `string.h`. This package needs only the two it gets.
//
// That was checked by running it rather than by reading the includes:
//
//     sysl build-lib . --target thumb-freestanding
//
// **What is NOT here is a `@link` directive**, and the absence is the point of vendoring: the
// implementation is compiled from the `.c` carried in this package, so there is no external library
// for the linker to be given.
//
// **Nor is there a `defines` block.** tiny_sha3 has two compile-time options, `KECCAKF_ROUNDS` and
// `ROTL64`, and both are `#ifndef`-guarded defaults that this package leaves exactly as upstream set
// them. `KECCAKF_ROUNDS` is 24 because FIPS 202 says Keccak-f[1600] has 24 rounds -- it is a knob for
// people studying reduced-round Keccak, and moving it would stop this being SHA-3.
